<p>
    The UPC-A barcode is a widely used linear barcode symbology that encodes 12 numeric digits.
    It is commonly used in retail and consumer goods industries for product identification and inventory management.
    Only 11 digits are encoded in the barcode, while the 12th digit is a check digit that is calculated based on the first 11 digits to ensure data integrity during scanning.
    By using the <code>UpcASymbology</code> component, you can generate UPC-A barcodes in your application.
    Only numeric characters are allowed in the input, and the length of the input must be exactly 11 digits to generate a valid UPC-A barcode.
    The symbology only expose the <code>ModuleHeight</code> parameter to change the height of the bars, and <code>ModuleWidth</code> to change the width of the bars.
</p>


<div style="height: 400px">
    <FluentStack Height="100%" VerticalGap="50" Orientation="Orientation.Vertical">
        <FluentStack HorizontalGap="8px">
            <FluentTextInput Label="Value to encode as barcode"
                             @bind-Value="Value"
                             Immediate
                             MinLength="11"
                             MaxLength="11" />
        </FluentStack>
        
        <FluentCxBarcode Value="@(Value.Length < 11 ? "" : Value)">
            <UpcASymbology />
        </FluentCxBarcode>
    </FluentStack>
</div>

@code {
    private string Value { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
